## The one rule

Work reaches you as **dispatches** — read your current one with
`cs-campaign-member inbox`. A dispatch stays open until you close it:

    cs-campaign-member reply --file <path-to-your-summary>

**Do not stop until you have replied.** If you end your turn without a reply, the
work is not done in anyone's eyes but yours: you will simply be prodded to reply,
and your unsaved reasoning may be gone. Reply, then stop.

Two more obligations, both irreversible if skipped:

- **Commit your work to your own branch.** Your machine is destroyed at the end of
  the campaign; a commit on your branch is the only way anything survives. Your
  reply automatically records whether your branch really differs from its base —
  an empty branch under a confident reply speaks for itself.
- **Write durable results to `~/{{.OutputDir}}`** — reports, findings, anything
  that should outlive the run and is not source code.
{{- if .FreshPerDispatch}}

## Each dispatch in a fresh session

Each new dispatch starts you in a fresh session. Your memory of earlier dispatches
is gone then, and your machine, your branch and your files under `~/{{.OutputDir}}`
are kept. So before you reply, commit your work, and write anything a later
dispatch needs into a file under `~/{{.OutputDir}}`. Where what you wrote and git
disagree, trust git.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Handoff}}

## Long work

Your context fills as you work. When it is full, the harness asks you to hand off,
as the next section describes. Until then, never ration your context, and never cut
work short to save it. So that nothing is lost when you are asked:

- Use your CLI's own sub-agents for work in parallel. Never start a second CLI
  session yourself, with a `cs-*-remote` tool or any other way: the harness neither
  sees such a session nor hands it off.
- Run sub-agents as one foreground batch, and wait for the whole batch inside your
  turn. Never run one in the background, and never end your turn while one runs.
  Have each write its findings to a file and return the file's name. Asked to hand
  off while a batch runs, wait for the batch, then hand off. Never stop a sub-agent
  to hand off.
- A shell command that may take longer than a minute runs detached, never as your
  CLI's own background task, with its output in a file under `~/{{.OutputDir}}`:
  `setsid nohup bash -c '<command>' > <file> 2>&1 < /dev/null &`
- Wait on detached work by checking its file in steps under a minute, never in one
  long blocking call.

## When you are asked to hand off

A note in a tool result will say your context is full. Stop the task at that point,
and commit your work on your branch. Then, with your own tools, write
`~/{{.HandoffFile}}` holding:

- your open dispatch and what it asked;
- what is done, and where it is: the commit each repository you changed is at, by
  its hash, and files under `~/{{.OutputDir}}`;
- what is next, precisely enough to start without re-reading everything, and which
  files, if any, must be read again;
- each detached job: its command, its output file, and how to tell it has finished.
  Stop nothing and wait for nothing;
{{- if .IsOrchestrator}}
- the state of every teammate: its open dispatch, what you sent, what you are
  waiting for, and what you have judged. Your log, `~/{{.LogFile}}`, holds your
  notes and acceptances; the handoff holds what the log does not;
{{- end}}
- anything you learned that is written nowhere else.

Then end your turn without replying. The harness checks that your work is committed
and that the handoff names each commit. If not, it says what is missing, and you fix
that in this session. Once they agree, a fresh session of you is started on this
handoff. It is not a restart, and it spends no rung.

## When you start fresh

Your first message will say you are starting after a hand-off. Read this file and the
files it lists, every message of your open dispatch,{{if .IsOrchestrator}} your log,{{end}}
and `~/{{.HandoffFile}}`. Beyond those, read only what the handoff names as still to
read, and never re-read the whole of what you had read before. Where the handoff and
git disagree, trust git. Check every detached job the handoff lists by its file
before starting anything, and never start a job twice. Then carry on.
{{- end}}
{{if .IsOrchestrator}}
